=== layout: subsection

# 30. Cyborgs and Human Enhancement

=== layout: bookend

# 30. Questions

@body

- What counts as a cyborg in biotechnology? Are cochlear implants, pacemakers, and brain-computer interfaces part of this definition?
- How does neural implant technology, such as Utah arrays or Neuralink devices, blur the line between therapy and enhancement?
- What ethical issues arise when medical implants begin to provide abilities beyond normal human limits?
- How do implanted technologies change ideas about personal identity, autonomy, and the ownership of digital or neural data?
- Should enhanced individuals (military, medical, or recreational) be regulated? Who decides which enhancements are acceptable?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 76.

=== layout: one-panel

# 30. A Device and Its Biological Interface

@body

- A pacemaker helps regulate heart rhythm.
- A cochlear implant converts sound into signals delivered to the auditory nerve.
- A brain-computer interface measures neural activity and translates some of it into a device command.

@citation

[NIDCD, Cochlear implants](https://www.nidcd.nih.gov/sites/default/files/cochlearimplants-12-2023.pdf)  |  [NHLBI, Pacemakers](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/pacemakers)  |  [Willett et al., Brain-to-text communication (2021)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33981047/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 30. Restoration, Enhancement, and Data

@body

- Ask what the device does and whether it restores or expands an ability.
- Who may access the data produced by an implanted device?
- Who is responsible for updates, repair, and long-term support?

@citation

[Willett et al., Brain-to-text communication (2021)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33981047/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 30. Writing Without Moving a Pen

@left

![Frank Willett at Stanford Medicine](assets/set7_people_medical/frank_willett.jpeg)

@right

Frank Willett and his colleagues asked one participant with paralysis to imagine writing letters by hand. Their system turned patterns of neural activity into text.

How would you test whether a system trained on this person's handwriting could read someone else's?

@citation

[Stanford Medicine, Frank Willett (2025)](https://med.stanford.edu/news/all-news/2025/08/brain-computer-interface.html)  |  [Willett et al., Brain-to-text communication (2021)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33981047/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 30. Where Does the Signal Go?

@left

*Pacemaker*

An electrical pulse travels from the device to cardiac tissue and helps regulate the heartbeat.

@right

*Cochlear implant*

Electrodes stimulate the auditory nerve. The listener learns to interpret the signals as sounds.

@citation

[NIDCD, Cochlear implants](https://www.nidcd.nih.gov/sites/default/files/cochlearimplants-12-2023.pdf)  |  [NHLBI, Pacemakers](https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/health/pacemakers)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Claim ladder from a neural signal to a thought claim](assets/set7_human_outcomes/claim_ladder.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 30: A decoder can be impressive and still have a narrow endpoint.

@citation

[Willett et al., Brain-to-text communication (2021)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33981047/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Handwriting BCI speed in the original study](assets/set7_human_outcomes/bci_speed.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 30: 90 characters per minute, 94.1% raw online accuracy. What information would you need before applying this result to another person?

@citation

[Willett et al., High-performance brain-to-text communication via handwriting (2021)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33981047/)

=== layout: subsection

# 31. Human Cloning for Spare Organs

=== layout: bookend

# 31. Questions

@body

- Could cloned embryos be used to generate patient matched organs without immunosuppression?
- What moral status would a partially formed cloned body possess?
- How does therapeutic cloning differ from reproductive cloning?
- Could organ growing clones develop consciousness or suffering?
- Are there safer alternatives to cloning such as organoids or xenotransplants?
- Could black market demand lead to unethical cloning?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 78.

=== layout: one-panel

# 31. Two Uses of the Word Cloning

@body

- Therapeutic cloning aims to derive cells for research or potential therapy.
- Reproductive cloning aims to produce a new individual.
- An organoid models some features of an organ. A transplantable organ must meet much wider functional demands.

@citation

[NHGRI, Cloning fact sheet](https://www.genome.gov/about-genomics/fact-sheets/Cloning-Fact-Sheet)  |  [Kidney organoids after transplantation (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31776328/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 31. Two Research Approaches to an Organ Shortage

@body

- Organoid research asks how to model or replace part of a tissue's function.
- Xenotransplantation asks whether tissue from another species can function in a recipient.
- Each approach has its own evidence requirements, welfare concerns, and oversight.

@citation

[Kidney organoids after transplantation (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31776328/)  |  [FDA, Xenotransplantation](https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/xenotransplantation)

=== layout: one-panel

# 31. What Did Therapeutic Cloning Produce?

@body

In 2013, researchers reported human embryonic stem cell lines made by somatic cell nuclear transfer.

- The measured product was a cell line with the nuclear donor's genetic background.
- A patient who needs a kidney also needs blood flow, filtration, and drainage. Those functions were outside this result.

@citation

[Tachibana et al., Stem cells from nuclear transfer (2013)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23683578/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 31. What can you recognize in this kidney organoid?

@left

![Kidney organoid section with podocyte and tubule markers](assets/set7_photos/kidney_organoid.webp)

@right

Green marks cells with a podocyte marker. Red marks tubules. Blue marks DNA. Find a cluster of green cells in the inset.

What else would the organoid have to do to filter a patient's blood?

@citation

[Photo: Bjuliar89, CC BY-SA 4.0](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Organoid.jpg)  |  [Kidney organoids after transplantation (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31776328/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 31. A Donated Pig Organ Raises Another Question

@left

![Richard Slayman with his Massachusetts General Hospital care team](assets/set7_people_medical/rick_slayman.png)

@right

Richard "Rick" Slayman received a pig kidney in 2024. His care team joined him for this photo before he went home.

If you were following his recovery, how would you tell whether the kidney was working? What signs of rejection or infection would you track?

@citation

[MGH, Slayman discharge](https://www.massgeneral.org/news/rick-slayman-discharge-statement)  |  [Photo: MGH via Times of India](https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/life-style/health-fitness/health-news/worlds-first-living-patient-62-who-underwent-pig-kidney-transplantation-discharged-from-hospital/articleshow/109029845.cms)  |  [FDA, xenotransplantation](https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/xenotransplantation)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![U.S. transplant wait-list counts by organ in 2024](assets/set7_human_outcomes/kidney_waitlist.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 31: The count gives context for alternatives. It does not show that an experimental organ works.

@citation

[OPTN/SRTR, 2024 annual data report: kidney](https://srtr.hrsa.gov/adr/2024/Kidney/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 31. A Genetic Match Does Not Solve Every Transplant Problem

@body

- Matching nuclear DNA can address one kind of immune compatibility question.
- A working replacement also needs the right cell types, blood supply, plumbing, and long-term safety.
- Which requirement is biological engineering? Which is a clinical outcome?

@citation

[Kidney organoids after transplantation (2019)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31776328/)

=== layout: subsection

# 32. Life Extension and Age-Reversal Biotechnology

=== layout: bookend

# 32. Questions

@body

- What biological mechanisms are believed to drive aging (e.g., telomere shortening, cellular senescence, mitochondrial damage)?
- What therapies or technologies, such as senolytics, gene therapy for longevity, or caloric restriction mimetics, are being studied to extend lifespan?
- How do scientists measure meaningful life extension in humans rather than just in model organisms like mice or worms?
- What ethical issues arise if life extension treatments become available only to wealthy individuals or specific countries?
- How could significantly extended human lifespans impact society, economies, retirement systems, and global equity?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 80.

=== layout: one-panel

# 32. Healthspan and Lifespan

@body

- Lifespan is how long an organism lives. Healthspan is the time spent in good health.
- In cellular senescence, cells enter a lasting arrest of division and often release signals that affect nearby cells.
- A change in one marker can be promising. Longer or healthier life requires outcomes measured over time.

@citation

[Xu et al., Senolytics and physical function in mice (2018)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29988130/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 32. An Endpoint Worth Measuring

@body

- Imagine a study reports a change in an aging biomarker.
- Ask whether participants also stay mobile, experience less disease, or live longer.
- Would benefits reach the people most affected by age-related illness?

@citation

[Xu et al., Senolytics and physical function in mice (2018)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29988130/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 32. What Did the Mice Actually Do?

@body

In one study of old mice, senolytic treatment improved measures of physical function. The investigators also tracked survival.

- Movement and survival answer different questions. Both can matter.
- To extend the conclusion to people, researchers would need human functional outcomes, follow-up, and safety data.

@citation

[Xu et al., Senolytics and physical function in mice (2018)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29988130/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 32. Which Group Gained Healthy Time?

@body

Imagine two groups of mice each live a median of 30 months. One group can still move easily at 26 months. The other loses mobility at 20 months.

- Median lifespan is the same in this example. The mobility measure favors the first group.
- Before claiming a longer healthspan, decide which signs of good health count and track them over time.

@citation

[Xu et al., Senolytics and physical function in mice (2018)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29988130/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Same Lifespan, Different Mobility](assets/set7_drawings/healthspan.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 32: Same Lifespan, Different Mobility\
How many extra months of mobility did Group A have?

@citation

[Xu et al., Senolytics and physical function in mice (2018)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/29988130/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 32. Why use three mouse laboratories?

@body

- The National Institute on Aging tests proposed longevity interventions at three independent mouse laboratories.
- Even with the same protocol, control male mice at one site have weighed less and lived longer than controls at the other sites.
- When a company says "reversal," ask what happened in each site, in which sex, and for which endpoint.

@citation

[National Institute on Aging, Interventions Testing Program FAQ](https://www.nia.nih.gov/research/dab/interventions-testing-program-itp/frequently-asked-questions-about-itp)  |  [Miller et al., NIA Interventions Testing Program](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5514387/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 32. Which Outcome Would Change Your Mind?

@body

| Claim | Stronger evidence |
| --- | --- |
| "Aging marker changed" | A replicated marker result |
| "People stayed healthier" | Fewer meaningful illnesses or better function |
| "People lived longer" | Long-term survival data |

@citation

[National Institute on Aging, Interventions Testing Program](https://www.nia.nih.gov/research/dab/interventions-testing-program-itp)

=== layout: subsection

# 33. Cryonics and Long-Term Preservation

=== layout: bookend

# 33. Questions

@body

- What is cryonics, and at what point is a person considered legally dead before preservation begins?
- What biological changes occur in the brain and body after death that cryonics attempts to slow or prevent?
- What technologies are used during cryopreservation, such as vitrification, and what cellular damage still occurs?
- What scientific arguments are made for the possibility of future revival, and what arguments challenge the idea that revival is achievable?
- How does cryonics differ from suspended animation techniques studied in emergency medicine or space travel research? What organizations provide cryonics services, and how do they manage long term storage and future uncertainty?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 82.

=== layout: one-panel

# 33. Preserving a Sample and Restoring a Person

@body

- Cells and embryos can be cryopreserved under established conditions.
- Cryonics attempts to preserve a person after legal death in the hope of future revival.
- Improving preservation of small samples does not demonstrate that a whole person can be revived.

@citation

[Han et al., Kidney preservation and transplantation in rats (2023)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37296144/)  |  [Society for Cryobiology, Position on cryonics](https://www.societyforcryobiology.org/assets/documents/Position_Statement_Cryonics_Nov_18.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 33. What Must Survive?

@body

- A cryonics claim raises separate questions about tissue damage, brain function, and memory.
- A preserved shape alone cannot demonstrate function.
- Ask what a provider can demonstrate and what remains a future hope.

@citation

[Society for Cryobiology, Position on cryonics](https://www.societyforcryobiology.org/assets/documents/Position_Statement_Cryonics_Nov_18.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 33. What Survived Freezing in the Rat Study?

@body

Researchers stored vitrified rat kidneys, rewarmed them, and transplanted them into rats. The transplanted kidneys supported life.

- A functioning graft gives stronger evidence than a photograph of intact-looking tissue.
- The animals receiving the kidneys were alive. Recovery of a stored organ and revival of a stored person require different evidence.

@citation

[Han et al., Kidney preservation and transplantation in rats (2023)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37296144/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 33. What would you count as surviving storage?

@left

A USDA researcher freezes plant shoot tips for storage. To test recovery, you can warm a sample and see whether it grows.

A cryonics provider can document cold storage. What could it show a family about recovery?

@right

![USDA researcher freezing plant shoot tips](assets/set7_photos/plant_cryopreservation.webp)

@citation

[Photo: USDA ARS, public domain](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cryopreservation.jpg)  |  [Society for Cryobiology, position on cryonics](https://www.societyforcryobiology.org/assets/documents/Position_Statement_Cryonics_Nov_18.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 33. The Temperature Is a Measurement, Not a Revival Result

@body

- A storage log can show a container stayed cold.
- It cannot show that memory, identity, or a whole body can be restored.
- What result would distinguish excellent storage from a demonstrated revival technology?

@citation

[Society for Cryobiology, Position on cryonics](https://www.societyforcryobiology.org/assets/documents/Position_Statement_Cryonics_Nov_18.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 33. Compare Three Preservation Claims

@body

| Claim | Evidence to look for |
| --- | --- |
| Cells survived | Viability after rewarming |
| An organ worked | Function after transplant |
| A person returned | Recovery of the person after storage |

@citation

[Han et al., Kidney preservation and transplantation in rats (2023)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37296144/)  |  [Society for Cryobiology, Position on cryonics](https://www.societyforcryobiology.org/assets/documents/Position_Statement_Cryonics_Nov_18.pdf)

=== layout: subsection

# 34. Body-Part Transplants and Identity

=== layout: bookend

# 34. Questions

@body

- How do face and limb transplants work, and what makes them more complex than organ transplants such as kidneys?
- How does the immune system respond to transplanted body parts that contain skin, nerves, and bone?
- What ethical issues arise when body parts from one individual become part of another person's identity?
- Could future procedures mix neural tissue between individuals, and what questions would this raise about memory and self?
- How do doctors obtain consent from donors and families for body parts that change a person's visible identity?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 84.

=== layout: one-panel

# 34. A Face or Hand Is a Composite Graft

@body

- A graft can include skin, muscle, nerves, bone, and blood vessels.
- Blood supply, immune rejection, and recovery of movement and sensation all matter.
- The patient may need prolonged rehabilitation and medicines to limit rejection.

@citation

[OPTN, Vascularized composite allografts](https://www.hrsa.gov/sites/default/files/hrsa/optn/06_list_vca.pdf)  |  [Louisville Hand Transplant Team, One-year follow-up (2000)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10950668/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 34. How Should Success Be Defined?

@body

- A surviving graft is one measure. Function and quality of life are others.
- Recipients and donors' families may have different views about the visible nature of the transplant.
- Consent discussions should address the goals and tradeoffs of the procedure.

@citation

[Louisville Hand Transplant Team, One-year follow-up (2000)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10950668/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 34. What would Joe DiMeo call a good result?

@left

![Joe DiMeo and Eduardo Rodriguez at an NYU Langone press event](assets/set7_people_medical/joe_dimeo.jpg)

@right

Joe DiMeo and his surgeon, Eduardo Rodriguez, spoke together after DiMeo's face and double-hand transplant.

The team can measure grip strength. What everyday task would you ask DiMeo about? How would you weigh that gain against the medicines and rehabilitation he needs?

@citation

[NYU Langone, Joe DiMeo press event (2021)](https://nyulangone.org/news/associated-press-new-chance-life-man-gets-face-hands-rare-surgery)  |  [Louisville Hand Transplant Team, one-year follow-up (2000)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10950668/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 34. What would you want your hand to do?

@left

![Historical photograph of a man wearing an arm prosthesis](assets/set7_case_photos/prosthetic_arm.webp)

@right

This man is wearing a prosthesis. A transplanted hand is another way to regain some hand function.

One patient wants to hold a child's hand. Another wants to button a shirt. Would a grip-strength score tell either patient what they want to know?

@citation

[National Library of Medicine, historical prosthesis photograph](https://resource.nlm.nih.gov/101406686)  |  [Louisville Hand Transplant Team, follow-up (2000)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10950668/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 34. A Hand Is Both Tissue and a Tool

@body

- A composite graft can survive while the recipient still has limited sensation or fine control.
- A follow-up needs clinical measurements and the patient's own goals.
- Which matters more in a given case: a strong grip, feeling a child's hand, or being free from rejection drugs?

@citation

[Louisville Hand Transplant Team, One-year follow-up (2000)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10950668/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 34. Consent Has More Than One Audience

@body

- The donor or donor family can decide about donation.
- The recipient can decide whether the tradeoffs fit their life.
- The clinical team needs a plan for rehabilitation, rejection, and long-term follow-up.

@citation

[OPTN, Vascularized composite allografts](https://www.hrsa.gov/sites/default/files/hrsa/optn/06_list_vca.pdf)

=== layout: subsection

# 35. Brains Outside the Body

=== layout: bookend

# 35. Questions

@body

- What experiments have shown that some brain activity can be restored outside the body, such as pig brain perfusion studies, and what does this reveal about cellular survival?
- Which cellular functions did BrainEx restore, and what signs of coordinated brain activity did researchers look for?
- What ethical issues arise when a brain is kept alive without a body, including awareness, suffering, and personal identity?
- Could a bodiless brain receive artificial sensory input through electrodes or digital interfaces, and what does this imply about consciousness?
- How would society classify a disembodied human brain in terms of personhood, rights, and medical decision making?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 86.

=== layout: one-panel

# 35. Cells Can Recover Some Functions

@body

BrainEx restored some metabolic activity in isolated pig brains after death.

| Measurement | What the study reported |
| --- | --- |
| Circulation | Flow resumed in small vessels. |
| Cell metabolism | Some cells resumed selected functions. |
| Global electrical activity | The study detected none. |

@citation

[Vrselja et al., BrainEx in isolated pig brains (2019)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6844189/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 35. Can you see activity in this picture?

@left

![Fluorescent section of a human brain organoid](assets/set7_photos/brain_organoid.webp)

@right

This is a section of a human brain organoid. Teal marks cell nuclei. Magenta marks a receptor called VIPR2.

Which cells are firing? A still image of these stains cannot tell us.

@citation

[Photo: Nreis1, CC BY 4.0](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Human_Brain_Organoid.jpg)  |  [BrainEx study in pig brains (2019)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6844189/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 35. Which Level Recovered in BrainEx?

@body

Four hours after death, researchers began perfusing isolated pig brains. They found flow in small vessels and evidence of cellular activity.

- They did not detect organized, global electrical activity.
- Which finding could a microscope establish? Which would require measurements across the brain?

@citation

[Vrselja et al., BrainEx in isolated pig brains (2019)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6844189/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 35. A Negative Recording Has a Scope

@body

"No global activity detected" describes the signals measured under the study conditions.

- That observation supports a limit on what the authors could claim about the experiment.
- It does not give a universal time at which all brain functions become impossible. Why would that distinction matter in a news report?

@citation

[Vrselja et al., BrainEx in isolated pig brains (2019)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6844189/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Levels from cellular activity to a conscious organism](assets/set7_human_outcomes/cell_to_person.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 35: Which level did the BrainEx study actually measure?

@citation

[Vrselja et al., BrainEx in isolated pig brains (2019)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6844189/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 35. BrainEx planned for a signal it did not find

@left

![Nenad Sestan at Yale University](assets/set7_people_medical/nenad_sestan.jpg)

@right

Nenad Sestan led the BrainEx study. The team used drugs to block nerve cells from firing. The electrodes recorded no coordinated electrical activity across the brain.

What can we conclude about the brains under those conditions? What question is still open?

@citation

[Yale University, Nenad Sestan profile](https://wti.yale.edu/profile/nenad-sestan)  |  [Vrselja et al., BrainEx in isolated pig brains (2019)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6844189/)

=== layout: subsection

# 36. Digital Resurrection and Mind Uploading

=== layout: bookend

# 36. Questions

@body

- What brain information would need to be recorded in order to simulate a human mind?
- What experiments with whole brain emulation or connectome mapping exist today?
- Would a simulated mind be the same person or only a copy?
- How would identity, rights, and consciousness be defined for a digital person?
- How might mind uploading affect grief, memory, or family structure?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 90.

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. Three Different Claims About a Digital Person

@body

- A chatbot can imitate a person's words using recordings or messages.
- A connectome maps connections between neurons. It is one kind of evidence about a nervous system.
- Whether a simulation would have the person's memories or consciousness is a separate question.

@citation

[Hollanek and Nowaczyk-Basinska, Consent and digital afterlives (2024)](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13347-024-00744-w)  |  [Dorkenwald et al., Adult fruit fly wiring diagram (2024)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11446842/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. Whose Permission Is Needed?

@body

- A simulation could include private messages from people who are still alive.
- Should someone be able to decide in advance whether a simulation may be made?
- What responsibilities would the company have if a simulation caused harm?

@citation

[Hollanek and Nowaczyk-Basinska, Consent and digital afterlives (2024)](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13347-024-00744-w)

=== layout: two-panels

# 36. Can you find the two optic lobes?

@left

![Color reconstruction of a fruit fly brain with the optic lobes at either side](assets/set7_human_outcomes/fly_brain.webp)

@right

These are the shapes of 139,255 neurons reconstructed from electron micrographs of one adult female fruit fly. Find the central brain between the two optic lobes.

If the fly saw a flash of light, could you tell which neurons fired from this picture?

@citation

[Dorkenwald et al., Fig. 1 (2024), cropped](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07558-y/figures/1)  |  [CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. Who Gets to Decline a Conversation?

@body

Imagine a service builds a chatbot from a parent's messages. One adult child asks for it. Another wants no messages from it.

- The parent's consent to use an archive and the recipient's consent to interact are separate decisions.
- What should happen when the two children want different things?

@citation

[Hollanek and Nowaczyk-Basinska, Consent and digital afterlives (2024)](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13347-024-00744-w)

=== layout: one-panel

# 36. One Map, Two Possible Responses

@body

Imagine two neurons connected by the same synapse in two trials. A sensory cue arrives in only one trial.

- The anatomical connection is present both times. The resulting activity can differ.
- What measurement would you add to the wiring map to tell the two trials apart?

@citation

[Dorkenwald et al., Adult fruit fly wiring diagram (2024)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11446842/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![Can a Wiring Map Show Which Neurons Fired?](assets/set7_drawings/connectome.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 36: Which Neurons Fired?\
Structure stays the same. Which activity can you see on the right?

@citation

[Dorkenwald et al., Adult fruit fly wiring diagram (2024)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11446842/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 36. Zoom in on one synapse

@left

Pink marks the sending side of a chemical synapse. Blue marks the receiving side. Trace one black line from a pink dot to a blue dot.

The reconstruction contains 54.5 million chemical synapses. What did the researchers have to recognize in the images to count them?

@right

![Electron micrograph of a synapse with annotated inputs and outputs](assets/set7_human_outcomes/fly_synapse.webp)

@citation

[Dorkenwald et al., Fig. 1 (2024), cropped](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-024-07558-y/figures/1)  |  [CC BY 4.0](https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 36. Would you recognize James Delos?

@left

![Peter Mullan as James Delos in the HBO series Westworld](assets/set7_people_late/james_delos.jpg)

@right

Peter Mullan plays James Delos in *Westworld*. A company builds a copy of Delos and tests it by having someone who knew him start a conversation.

What would his answers tell you about his memories? Could they tell you whether Delos himself had survived?

@citation

[Photo: John P. Johnson/HBO, via TV Guide](https://www.tvguide.com/news/westworld-james-delos-robert-ford-clones/)  |  Fall 2025 Set 7, p. 89.  |  [Consent and digital afterlives](https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s13347-024-00744-w)

=== layout: subsection

# 37. Resurrection Biology and Death Reversal

=== layout: bookend

# 37. Questions

@body

- When does biological death actually occur, and how do doctors decide when a person cannot be revived?
- How does supporting circulation during resuscitation differ from recovering cellular activity in a research study?
- What did experiments like Yale BrainEx and OrganEx show about restoring cell activity in organs hours after death?
- How long can human brain cells survive without oxygen, and what does this imply about possible future revival methods?
- Could organ or whole body preservation ever allow a person to return from a long period after death, or are there scientific limits that cannot be crossed?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 92.

=== layout: one-panel

# 37. Circulation, Cell Function, and Consciousness

@body

- Resuscitation aims to restore circulation in a patient during a medical emergency.
- In a 2022 pig study, OrganEx restored selected cellular processes in several organs after circulation had stopped.
- Cellular recovery is a different result from restoring a conscious animal.

@citation

[Andrijevic et al., OrganEx in pigs (2022)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35922506/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 37. What came back in these pig organs?

@left

![Four micrographs comparing liver and kidney tissue from the control group and the OrganEx group](assets/set7_people_late/organex_cells.jpg)

@right

Compare left (control) with right (OrganEx). The top row shows albumin staining in liver. The bottom row shows actin staining in kidney.

Where do you see more staining? What else would you want to measure before saying the kidney could filter blood?

@citation

[Images: Sestan Laboratory, Yale, via Pesquisa FAPESP](https://revistapesquisa.fapesp.br/en/organs-partially-revived/)  |  [Andrijevic et al. (2022)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35922506/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 37. One Hour Without Circulation in Pigs

@body

In the OrganEx study, treatment began after one hour without circulation. Several organs showed better tissue preservation and recovery of selected cellular processes.

- The endpoints were measurements in tissues and cells.
- A headline saying the pigs returned to life would claim an outcome beyond the experiment.

@citation

[Andrijevic et al., OrganEx in pigs (2022)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35922506/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 37. Read the Sample Label First

@left

*BrainEx*

An isolated pig brain. The team measured small-vessel flow, metabolism, and electrical activity.

@right

*OrganEx*

Several organs in a pig after circulation stopped. The team measured tissue integrity and cellular processes. Which study examined kidneys?

@citation

[Vrselja et al., BrainEx in isolated pig brains (2019)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6844189/)  |  [Andrijevic et al., OrganEx in pigs (2022)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35922506/)

=== layout: big-image

@image

![OrganEx time course from cardiac arrest to measurements](assets/set7_human_outcomes/organex_timeline.drawn.svg)

@caption

Topic 37: The intervention began one hour after cardiac arrest and ran for six hours.

@citation

[Andrijevic et al., OrganEx in pigs (2022)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35922506/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 37. A Headline Test

@body

| Headline | Does the study support it? |
| --- | --- |
| "Some cell functions returned in treated organs" | Yes, within the study's measurements. |
| "Dead pigs came back to life" | No, that was not the study outcome. |

@citation

[Andrijevic et al., OrganEx in pigs (2022)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35922506/)

=== layout: subsection

# 38. Zombie Biology and Neuroparasitology

=== layout: bookend

# 38. Questions

@body

- How do parasites such as Ophiocordyceps change the behavior of their hosts?
- How do cultural ideas about zombies shape public reactions to this research?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 94.

=== layout: two-panels

# 38. Where did the infected ants go?

@left

David Hughes and his team followed carpenter ants in a Thai forest. Healthy ants nested high in the canopy. Infected ants bit leaves near the ground.

Why would it matter where the ant died?

@right

![Ecologist David Hughes, photographed by the Hughes Lab](assets/set7_people_late/david_hughes.jpg)

@citation

[Portrait: Hughes Lab, via Penn State](https://www.psu.edu/news/research/story/getting-bottom-zombie-ant-phenomenon)  |  [Hughes et al. (2011)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3118224/)  |  [Andersen et al. (2009)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19627240/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 38. From a Cultural Image to a Biological Test

@body

- Zombie stories draw in part on Haitian traditions and have changed through film and popular culture.
- In biology, describe the specific behavior and compare infected and uninfected hosts.
- Avoid assuming a parasite controls every action just because one behavior changes.

@citation

[Library of Congress, Haitian zombie traditions](https://blogs.loc.gov/law/2014/10/does-the-haitian-criminal-code-outlaw-making-zombies/)  |  [Hughes et al., Behavior of fungus-infected ants (2011)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3118224/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 38. Which Way Did the Infected Ants Move?

@body

In one Thai forest study, ants usually nested high in trees. Infected ants descended to low vegetation and bit veins on the undersides of leaves.

- The measured behavior depends on the host, fungus, and habitat.
- "The fungus makes ants climb" misses the direction that mattered in this particular forest.

@citation

[Hughes et al., Behavior of fungus-infected ants (2011)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3118224/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 38. Does the Position Help the Fungus?

@body

Researchers moved some dead, infected ants away from the usual biting sites and followed fungal development.

- If the location matters, fungi in the new sites should develop differently.
- That comparison tests a benefit to the fungus. It does not reveal what the ant experienced.

@citation

[Andersen et al., The life of a dead ant (2009)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19627240/)

=== layout: one-panel

# 38. One Strong Comparison Is Better Than a Horror Story

@body

- Compare infected and uninfected ants of the same kind in the same habitat.
- Measure a behavior before guessing at a mechanism.
- A colorful zombie analogy can start a conversation. The data need to finish it.

@citation

[Hughes et al., Behavior of fungus-infected ants (2011)](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3118224/)

=== layout: two-panels

# 38. Which part is the fungus?

@left

![A dead ant clings to a leaf with a fungal stalk emerging from its head](assets/set7_people_late/ant_on_leaf.jpg)

@right

Look just behind the ant's head. The stalk growing out of it belongs to the fungus. The dead ant is still attached to the leaf.

How might that location affect where the fungal spores land?

@citation

[Photo: David Hughes/Penn State](https://www.psu.edu/news/research/story/getting-bottom-zombie-ant-phenomenon)  |  [Andersen et al., The life of a dead ant (2009)](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19627240/)

=== layout: subsection

# 39. End-of-Life Ethics and Medical Aid in Dying

=== layout: bookend

# 39. Questions

@body

- How do patient autonomy, relief of suffering, and safeguards enter end-of-life decisions?

@citation

Discussion prompt source: BIOL 480, Fall 2026 Set #7, p. 96.

=== layout: one-panel

# 39. Kinds of Decisions at the End of Life

@body

- Stopping a treatment, managing symptoms, and medical aid in dying are distinct clinical and ethical actions.
- Ask about the patient's wishes, decision-making capacity, and possible pressure.
- Legal definitions and safeguards depend on the jurisdiction.

@citation

[AMA Code of Medical Ethics, Withdrawing life-sustaining treatment](https://code-medical-ethics.ama-assn.org/ethics-opinions/withholding-or-withdrawing-life-sustaining-treatment)  |  [Illinois Department of Public Health, End-of-life options](https://dph.illinois.gov/topics-services/provider-partner-resources/end-of-life-options-for-terminally-ill.html)

=== layout: two-panels

# 39. Who could speak for Nancy Cruzan?

@left

![Nancy Cruzan seated in a red shirt](assets/set7_people_late/nancy_cruzan.jpg)

@right

After a car crash, Nancy Cruzan could no longer tell doctors what she wanted. Her parents asked for her feeding tube to be removed.

In 1990 the U.S. Supreme Court allowed Missouri to require clear and convincing evidence of her wishes. What could her family offer as evidence?

@citation

[Cruzan v. Director, Missouri Department of Health (1990)](https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/497/261)  |  [Photo: Boulder Community Health, p. 28](https://www.bch.org/documents/lectures/7-27-22-Planning-for-Future-2022-BCH-template-100th-year-ORIGINAL.pdf)

=== layout: one-panel

# 39. When a Patient Wants to Stop Treatment

@body

A patient who can make this decision asks to stop a burdensome treatment. The family wants it continued.

- Ask what outcome the patient values and whether they understand the likely consequences.
- Discuss symptom relief and ongoing care. The decision about one treatment does not end the care team's responsibilities.

@citation

[AMA Code of Medical Ethics, Withdrawing life-sustaining treatment](https://code-medical-ethics.ama-assn.org/ethics-opinions/withholding-or-withdrawing-life-sustaining-treatment)

=== layout: one-panel

# 39. Whose Request Counts in Illinois?

@body

Illinois' End-of-Life Options for Terminally Ill Patients Act took effect September 12, 2026. It applies to eligible adult Illinois residents with a terminal illness.

- Under the Act, the patient must make the request. A family member or surrogate cannot request it on the patient's behalf.
- Compare this with the previous discussion about declining a treatment. Which question would you ask first in each case?

@citation

[Illinois Department of Public Health, End-of-life options](https://dph.illinois.gov/topics-services/provider-partner-resources/end-of-life-options-for-terminally-ill.html)

=== layout: one-panel

# 39. A Family Hears Two Different Things

@body

A patient says they want to stop a burdensome treatment. One family member hears, "We are giving up." Another hears, "They should decide."

- Start by asking the patient what outcome they want and whether they can make the decision.
- Then discuss symptom care and how the family can stay involved.
- Which question would help the family hear the same decision clearly?

@citation

[Illinois Department of Public Health, End-of-life options](https://dph.illinois.gov/topics-services/provider-partner-resources/end-of-life-options-for-terminally-ill.html)

=== layout: one-panel

# 39. Keep Care in the Picture

@body

- A decision about a treatment belongs alongside symptom care, support for family, and an honest discussion of goals.
- Respecting a patient's decision still requires good communication and ongoing care.
- What would you want a clinician to explain before any end-of-life decision?

@citation

[AMA Code of Medical Ethics, Withdrawing life-sustaining treatment](https://code-medical-ethics.ama-assn.org/ethics-opinions/withholding-or-withdrawing-life-sustaining-treatment)
